Version 0.2  (unreleased)
	Add imcat --info, which reports whether the terminal supports
	  SIXEL, how many colour registers it has, how big it is, the
	  largest image it will draw, and what imcat would use
	Ask the terminal how many SIXEL colour registers it has
	  (XTSMGRAPHICS) and quantize to that many instead of always
	  assuming 255, so terminals configured with more (xterm allows up
	  to 1024) get a finer palette
	Add imcat --max-colours/--max-colors and the SYXEL_MAX_COLOURS
	  environment variable, which also applies to the matplotlib backend
	Grow the fixed fallback cube with the palette, from 5x9x5 to 8x15x8
	When the most frequent colours do not cover the image, round the
	  colours (dropping low bits from blue, then red, then green, one
	  bit at a time) until they do, instead of falling back to the
	  fixed RGB cube, which puts the colour registers where the image
	  actually uses them

Version 0.1  2026-08-19 (first release)
	Convert images to SIXEL and write them to the terminal
	Add the imcat command line tool, with --max-height/--max-width
	  subsampling limits and --version
	Add a matplotlib backend (syxel.backend_sixel), scaling figures to
	  the terminal and registering a sixel savefig format
	Quantize to at most 256 colour registers, falling back to a fixed
	  5x9x5 RGB cube when the most frequent colours do not cover the image
	Run-length encode the output
	Report image files that cannot be read (missing, unreadable, or not
	  an image) as an error message rather than a traceback, skipping
	  them and exiting with a non-zero status
	Make imread (imcat extra) and matplotlib (matplotlib extra) optional;
	  numpy is the only hard dependency
